## Problem Interpretation

The problem asks for the automorphism (isomorphism) group of *the* directed graph whose underlying undirected graph is $K_{3,3}$. The most canonical such directed graph is the **bidirected** $K_{3,3}$: replace each undirected edge $\{u,v\}$ by a pair of oppositely directed arcs $u\to v$ and $v\to u$. This is the unique directed graph (up to isomorphism) that "becomes $K_{3,3}$ when all edges are made undirected" **and** carries both directions on every edge—making it the natural directed analogue of $K_{3,3}$.

---

## Proof

Let $D$ be the bidirected $K_{3,3}$ with vertex set $V = A \cup B$, where $A = \{a_1, a_2, a_3\}$, $B = \{b_1, b_2, b_3\}$, and for every $i,j$ there are arcs $a_i \to b_j$ and $b_j \to a_i$.

**Step 1: Reduce to the undirected automorphism group.**

Since $D$ contains both arcs $u\to v$ and $v\to u$ whenever $u,v$ are adjacent, a vertex permutation $\varphi$ is an automorphism of $D$ if and only if it preserves adjacency in the underlying undirected graph. Hence

$$\operatorname{Aut}(D) = \operatorname{Aut}(K_{3,3}).$$

**Step 2: The bipartition is uniquely determined.**

$K_{3,3}$ is connected and bipartite, so its proper 2-coloring is unique up to swapping the two color classes. Thus the bipartition $\{A, B\}$ is an invariant of the graph: every automorphism either maps $A\to A,\; B\to B$ (preserves parts) or maps $A\to B,\; B\to A$ (swaps parts).

**Step 3: Automorphisms preserving each part.**

For any $\sigma \in S_3$ and $\pi \in S_3$, the map
$$a_i \mapsto a_{\sigma(i)}, \qquad b_j \mapsto b_{\pi(j)}$$
sends every edge $\{a_i, b_j\}$ to $\{a_{\sigma(i)}, b_{\pi(j)}\}$, which is again an edge. This gives a subgroup
$$S_3 \times S_3 \;\leq\; \operatorname{Aut}(K_{3,3}).$$

**Step 4: The part-swapping automorphism.**

The map $\tau: a_i \mapsto b_i,\; b_j \mapsto a_j$ sends edge $\{a_i,b_j\}$ to $\{b_i, a_j\} = \{a_j, b_i\}$, which is an edge. So $\tau \in \operatorname{Aut}(K_{3,3})$, and $\tau$ has order 2.

**Step 5: Semidirect product structure.**

Conjugation by $\tau$ swaps the two $S_3$ factors:
$$\tau\,(\sigma,\pi)\,\tau^{-1} = (\pi, \sigma).$$
The preserving-parts subgroup $S_3 \times S_3$ has index 2 (since every automorphism either preserves or swaps parts), so it is normal, and
$$\operatorname{Aut}(K_{3,3}) = (S_3 \times S_3) \rtimes \langle \tau \rangle \cong (S_3 \times S_3) \rtimes C_2.$$

**Step 6: Identify the group.**

This is exactly the **wreath product** $S_3 \wr C_2$, of order
$$|S_3|^2 \cdot |C_2| = 6^2 \cdot 2 = 72.$$

---

## Answer

The isomorphism group of the directed graph is

$$\boxed{\operatorname{Aut}(K_{3,3}) \;\cong\; (S_3 \times S_3) \rtimes C_2 \;\cong\; S_3 \wr C_2},$$

the wreath product of $S_3$ by $C_2$, of order **72**.

